 "about": {
  "text": "Wild, backward, doomed: For decades, cinema portrayed Indigenous cultures with such clichés—if they were addressed at all. White directors shaped the image of Indigenous people as the ultimate “others.” A well-known example is *NANOOK OF THE NORTH* (1922), the first feature-length documentary to achieve commercial success. It is now well documented that director Robert J. Flaherty deliberately staged and distorted the lives of the Inuit. However, the film also shows that Canada’s Indigenous peoples were present on screen from the very beginning—though not as the authors of their own stories.",
  "lang": "en",
  "venue": "Xenix",
  "url": "https://www.xenix.ch/programm/november-2026/reclaiming-youth--junge-indigene-stimmen-aus-kanada",
  "translated": true
 },
